  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 313 PM EDT Friday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Confidence increases for the potential of severe \nstorms this afternoon and into this evening.\n\nAfternoon weather analysis shows a weak 500mb shortwave moving over \nthe area. While at the surface, a cold front is located along the \nPA/MD border. South of the front a hot and humid airmass is in place \nwith temperatures in the middle to upper 90s and low 100s across VA \nand NC and dew points in the upper 60s across the piedmont and low \nto middle 70s along the coast. With the approaching cold front the \nthreat for strong to severe thunderstorms increase as the atmosphere \nremains primed. Recent Meso-Analysis is showing strong Dcape (1200-\n1400J/kg) in place and Bulk-shear of around 30-40kt. This \nenvironment is suitable for strong to severe wind gusts of 60-70mph+ \nespecially given the strong DCAPE in place. However, severe hail \ncannot be ruled out with the a stronger and or more discrete storm \ngiven the shear in place. As of 230pm there are multiple areas of \ninterest for developing storms. The first place is across SE VA and \nNE NC where a remnant outflow boundary lays from this mornings MCS. \nCumulus field has quickly developed here in the past hour and \nreturns have been noted on radar. If these storms are able to tap \ninto the primed ATM they can potentially become severe. The second \narea of interest is across the piedmont as storms are firing along \nthe higher terrain to the west. Based on the ATM in place if these \nstorms will be in favorable environment  for initially discrete \nstorms then the potential formation of an MCS this evening. Will \nnote there remains a question of destabilization along and north of \nthe I-64 corridor. This is due to subsidence behind this mornings \nMCS. However, based on satellite trends and Meso-Analysis the \natmosphere looks to be recovering and becoming favorable for severe \nstorms late this afternoon and into the evening. With all of this \nbeing said a severe thunderstorm watch is in place through this \nevening.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Additional round of showers and thunderstorms are \npossible tomorrow.\n\nThere continues to remain some uncertainty regarding tomorrows \nshowers and thunderstorms for tomorrow as It heavily depends on what \nhappens this afternoon and evening. trough builds closer to the \nlocal area, pushing a cold front slowly through. While at the \nsurface, a cold front is expected to stall just south of the area. \nHowever, exact placement is yet unknown. The exact placement of the \nfront will help indicate where the highest severe threat will \nreside. As of this update the best chance for severe thunderstorms \ncontinue to be SE VA and NE NC where the highest chance for moderate \ncape and sufficient shear is expected to be colocated. The main risk \nwith these storms will be damaging wind, small to severe hail and \nlocalized flash flooding. Outside of the storm threat, this looks \nto be the last day of the late summer heat wave with temps in the \nmiddle to upper 80s and low 90s.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3... Below to around seasonable temps are likely late \nthis weekend into early next week before another brief heat up by \nthe middle of the week. \n\nAn upper level trough is likely to remain offshore helping to keep \nNW flow aloft. While at the surface, high pressure is progged to \nsit over New England allowing NE winds top prevail across the area. \nThis pattern looks to be favorable for around seasonable to perhaps a \ntad below seasonable temps. Then by middle of the week ensembles \nhint on an additional ridge building back into place favoring \nanother period of above average temps. In terms of rain chances, \nSunday looks to be the the last favorable day for a chance of \nshowers before a drier pattern returns by early next week.",
